red by the IC\'s `TSREF` output. These connect to the IC\'s GPIO pins via a configuration header (J4).\n     9\t4.  **Power & Communication:** The IC is powered from the battery stack (`PWR` net) and generates its own internal rails (`AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD`). It features a daisy-chainable communication interface (`COMH`/`COML`) and a local UART/SPI interface (`RX`/`TX`).\n    10\t5.  **Auxiliary Measurement Inputs:** The system includes two configurable paths for driving the `BBP` and `BBN` pins (Pins 64/63) of the BMS IC. These pins are typically used for auxiliary voltage measurements. The two options are:\n    11\t    *   **Bus Bar Measurement (DNP):** Measures the voltage difference between `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL` via a differential RC filter.\n    12\t    *   **Current Sense Measurement (Active):** Measures the voltage from `SRP_S` and `SRN_S` (likely current sense shunt taps). This path is currently populated (direct connection).\n    13\t6.  **Communication Interface:**\n    14\t    *   **Isolated Interface:** A digital isolator (U2) provides a galvanically isolated UART and Fault interface between the BMS IC (High Voltage) and an external host/microcontroller (Low Voltage).\n    15\t    *   **Direct Interface:** A header is provided for direct, non-isolated connection to the BMS UART pins.\n    16\t\n    17\t7.  **NPN Power Supply:** An external NPN transistor circuit is provided to regulate the `LDOIN` supply for the BMS IC, reducing power dissipation within the IC. This circuit is powered from the main `PWR` rail.\n    18\t\n    19\t# Components Inventory\n    20\t\n    21\t**Integrated Circuits**\n    22\t*   **U1:** TI BQ79616PAPQ1 (16-Channel Battery Monitor)\n    23\t*   **U2:** TI ISO7342CQDWRQ1 (Digital Isolator)\n    24\t\n    25\t**Transistors**\n    26\t*   **Q1:** NPN Transistor (External LDO Pass Element). Active.\n    27\t*   **Q2:** NPN Transistor footprint. DNP (Do Not Populate).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Power & Decoupling**\n    30\t*   **Input Filter:** R5 (30.0Ω), C5 (10nF) on `BAT` pin.\n    31\t*   **NPN Supply:**\n    32\t    *   **Input Path:** `PWR` connects to Q1 Collector via series resistors R4 (200Ω) and R3 (100Ω).\n    33\t    *   **Input Filter:** C1 (0.22μF) on the Collector of Q1.\n    34\t    *   **Regulation:** Q1 Emitter drives `LDOIN`. Q1 Base is driven directly by `NPNB`.\n    35\t*   **Internal Regulators:**\n    36\t    *   **AVDD/CVDD/DVDD:** Decoupled by C6 (1μF), C9 (1μF), C7 (4.7μF), C8 (1μF).\n    37\t    *   **LDOIN:** C59 (0.1μF).\n    38\t    *   **NEG5V:** C3 (0.1μF).\n    39\t    *   **TSREF:** C2 (1μF).\n    40\t    *   **Isolator Power:** C57 (0.1μF) on `USB2ANY_3.3V`, C58 (0.1μF) on `CVDD_CO`.\n    41\t*   **Indicator:** Green LED D1 with R121 (1.0k) and Jumper J6 (Power Indicator).\n    42\t\n    43\t**Resistors (Configuration Jumpers)**\n    44\t*   **Type:** 0-ohm links\n    45\t*   **Status:** All currently marked DNP (Do Not Populate)\n    46\t*   **Designators:**\n    47\t    *   **CB Series:** R21, R22, R25, R26\n    48\t    *   **VC Series:** R23, R24, R27, R28\n    49\t    *   **Auxiliary Select:** R10, R13, R17 (DNP).\n    50\t\n    51\t**Auxiliary Measurement Components**\n    52\t*   **Bus Bar Filter:**\n    53\t    *   **Resistors:** R9, R12 (402Ω)\n    54\t    *   **Capacitor:** C10 (0.47μF)\n    55\t    *   **Test Points:** TP16, TP17\n    56\t*   **Current Sense Filter:**\n    57\t    *   **Capacitor:** C11 (0.47μF, DNP)\n    58\t    *   **Test Points:** TP18, TP19\n    59\t\n    60\t**Connectors & Jumpers**\n    61\t*   **J4:** 2x8 Header (GPIO Configuration). Connects GPIO pins to thermistor circuits.\n    62\t*   **J5:** 2-pin Header. Connects `TSREF` to `PULLUP`.\n    63\t*   **J17:** 2x5 Header (USB2ANY / MCU Interface).\n    64\t*   **J18:** 2-pin Header. Connects `CVDD` to `CVDD_CO` (Isolator Power).\n    65\t*   **J21:** 2-pin Header. Connects `RX_CO` (Isolator Output) to `RX` (BMS Input).\n    66\t*   **J1:** 2-pin Header. Connects `RX_C` (Direct Input) to `RX` (BMS Input).\n    67\t*   **J2:** 2-pin Header. Connects `NFAULT` to `NF_J` (Isolator Input).\n    68\t*   **J3:** 6-pin Header (Direct UART). Labeled "J3 Pin Description".\n    69\t\n    70\t**Test Points**\n    71\t*   **Power & Reference:**\n    72\t    *   TP1 (TSREF), TP2 (LDOIN), TP3 (NEG5V), TP4 (NPNB)\n    73\t    *   TP5 (CVDD), TP6 (AVDD), TP7 (REFHP), TP8 (BAT), TP9 (DVDD)\n    74\t*   **Ground:** TP13, TP14, TP15\n    75\t*   **Communication & Logic:** TP12 (RX), TP42 (TX), TP43 (NFAULT)\n    76\t*   **Auxiliary Sense:** TP10 (BBP), TP11 (BBN)\n    77\t\n    78\t**Temperature Sensing Components**\n    79\t*   **Pullup Resistors (10.0k):** R7, R8, R11, R14, R15, R16, R18, R19.\n    80\t*   **Thermistors (NTC):** RT1, RT2, RT3, RT4, RT5, RT6, RT7, RT8.\n    81\t*   **GPIO1 Filter:**\n    82\t    *   **R128:** 1.0k Resistor\n    83\t    *   **C60:** 1uF Capacitor\n    84\t    *   **D3:** 24V Dual Zener/TVS Diode\n    85\t*   **DNP Components:** R20 (100k), C12 (DNP) on RT8 channel.\n    86\t\n    87\t# Connectivity & Signal Flow\n    88\t\n    89\t**Main IC (U1) Connections:**\n    90\t*   **Cell Inputs:** `VC0` through `VC16` connect to the cell voltage sense lines.\n    91\t*   **Balancing:** `CB0` through `CB16` connect to the cell balance lines.\n    92\t*   **GPIOs:** `GPIO1` through `GPIO8` connect to the temperature sensing block (via J4).\n    93\t*   **Power Supply:**\n    94\t    *   `BAT` (Pin 1) is the high-voltage supply, filtered from the `PWR` net.\n    95\t    *   `AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD` are internal regulator outputs tied together and decoupled to GND.\n    96\t    *   `TSREF` (Pin 51) provides the reference voltage for the thermistors.\n    97\t    *   `LDOIN` (Pin 47), `NEG5V` (Pin 44), `NPNB` (Pin 48), `REFHP` (Pin 37) are additional regulator/reference pins.\n    98\t    *   **NPN Supply (Optional):** `LDOIN` is powered via an external NPN (Q1). `PWR` -> R4 -> R3 -> Q1(C). Q1(E) -> `LDOIN`. Base is driven by `NPNB`. C1 filters the Collector input.\n    99\t*   **Communication:**\n   100\t    *   **Daisy Chain:** `COMHP`/`COMHN` (High side) and `COMLP`/`COMLN` (Low side) for stacking multiple BMS ICs.\n   101\t    *   **Local Host:** `RX` (Pin 52) and `TX` (Pin 53) for communication with a microcontroller.\n   102\t    *   **Fault:** `NFAULT` (Pin 62) is an active-low fault output.\n   103\t*   **Grounding:**\n   104\t    *   `AVSS`, `CVSS`, `DVSS`, `REFHM`, and `PAD` are tied to the local GND.\n   105\t    *   **System Grounding:** A note specifies that "GND is tied to CELL0 at connector via a thick trace," establishing the reference potential for the low-voltage side.\n   106\t\n   107\t**Communication Interface Details:**\n   108\t*   **Isolated Path (via U2):**\n   109\t    *   **TX (MCU -> BMS):** `USB2ANY_TX_3.3` (J17-7) -> U2 (INB->OUTB) -> `RX_CO` -> J21 -> `RX` (U1-52).\n   110\t    *   **RX (BMS -> MCU):** `TX` (U1-53) -> U2 (INC->OUTC) -> `USB2ANY_RX_3.3` (J17-8).\n   111\t    *   **Fault (BMS -> MCU):** `NFAULT` (U1-62) -> J2 -> `NF_J` -> U2 (IND->OUTD) -> `NFAULT_C` (J17-3).\n   112\t    *   **Power:** U2 Isolated side powered by `USB2ANY_3.3V`. BMS side powered by `CVDD` via J18.\n   113\t*   **Direct Path (Non-Isolated):**\n   114\t    *   **J3 (Direct UART Header):**\n   115\t        *   Pin 1: GND.\n   116\t        *   Pin 2: `NFAULT` (Schematic) / "FAULTn" (Text).\n   117\t        *   Pin 3: `RX_C` (Schematic) / "RX" (Text).\n   118\t        *   Pin 4: `TX` (Schematic) / "TX" (Text).\n   119\t        *   **Conflict:** The text description for J3 pins 4 and 5 ("4 RX", "5 TX") contradicts the schematic wires (Pin 4 is `TX`, Pin 3 is `RX_C`). The schematic wires are assumed correct: Pin 3 is Input (RX), Pin 4 is Output (TX).\n   120\t    *   `RX` (BMS) can be driven from `RX_C` (J3-3) via R119 (100Ω) and J1.\n   121\t    *   `TX` (BMS) connects directly to J3-4.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Auxiliary Measurement Inputs (BBP/BBN):**\n   124\t*   **Pins:** `BBP` (Pin 64) and `BBN` (Pin 63) on U1.\n   125\t*   **Path 1 (Bus Bar - DNP):**\n   126\t    *   Inputs: `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL`.\n   127\t    *   Filtering: Differential RC filter formed by R9/R12 (402Ω) and C10 (0.47μF).\n   128\t    *   Connection: Connects to `BBP`/`BBN` via R10/R13 (0Ω). Currently DNP.\n   129\t*   **Path 2 (Current Sense - Active):**\n   130\t    *   Inputs: `SRP_S` and `SRN_S`.\n   131\t    *   Filtering: Optional C11 (0.47μF, DNP).\n   132\t    *   Connection: Directly wired to `BBP`/`BBN`. (Note: R17 is shown as DNP across the lines, likely a placeholder).\n   133\t    *   **Conflict Note:** The schematic shows both paths merging at the `BBP`/`BBN` nets. Since Path 1 resistors are DNP, Path 2 is the active source.\n   134\t\n   135\t**Cell Balance (CB) Configuration Path:**\n   136\t*   **R25** connects `CB12` to `CB13`\n   137\t*   **R21** connects `CB13` to `CB14`\n   138\t*   **R26** connects `CB14` to `CB15`\n   139\t*   **R22** connects `CB15` to `CB16`\n   140\t\n   141\t**Voltage Sense (VC) Configuration Path:**\n   142\t*   **R27** connects `VC12` to `VC13`\n   143\t*   **R23** connects `VC13` to `VC14`\n   144\t*   **R28** connects `VC14` to `VC15`\n   145\t*   **R24** connects `VC15` to `VC16`\n   146\t\n   147\tIn the current DNP state, all these lines (`CB12`-`CB16` and `VC12`-`VC16`) are electrically isolated from each other within this specific block.\n   148\t\n   149\t**Temperature Measurement Subsystem:**\n   150\t*   **Reference Power:** `TSREF` (Thermistor Reference) connects to the `PULLUP` rail via jumper **J5**.\n   151\t*   **Divider Network:** The `PULLUP` rail feeds 8 parallel voltage dividers. Each divider consists of a 10.0k top resistor (e.g., R7) and a bottom NTC thermistor (e.g., RT1) to GND.\n   152\t*   **Measurement Points:** The midpoint of each divider is labeled `GPIOx_R` (e.g., `GPIO8_R`, `GPIO7_R`... `GPIO1_R`).\n   153\t*   **GPIO Interface (J4):**\n   154\t    *   **J4** allows connecting the BMS IC\'s `GPIOx` pins (odd pins 1-15) to the measurement points.\n   155\t    *   **Channels 2-8:** `GPIOx` connects directly to `GPIOx_R` when the jumper is placed.\n   156\t    *   **Channel 1:** Unlike channels 2-8, Channel 1 includes an intermediate filter stage. The divider node `GPIO1_R` (with C60 and D3) connects via R128 (1k) to `GPIO1_C`. `GPIO1_C` then connects to J4 Pin 2. Thus, the BMS pin `GPIO1` measures the filtered divider voltage through a 1k series resistor.\n   157\t    *   **TP44** provides a test point on the `GPIO1_R` net (the divider node).\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t# Uncertainties, Assumptions & Confidence\n   161\t\n   162\t*   **Assumption:** The numbers 12 through 16 in the configuration block represent the cell index in the stack.\n   163\t*   **Uncertainty:** The destination of the vertical lines (`CBxx`/`VCxx`) is now confirmed to be U1 on one side, but the connection to the actual battery connector/filter components is not yet visible.\n   164\t*   **Uncertainty:** Why only GPIO1 has the explicit filter/protection circuit (R128, C60, D3) shown.\n   165\t*   **Uncertainty:** The exact nature of D3 (24V). It appears to be a bidirectional TVS or Zener pair for overvoltage protection.\n   166\t*   **Resolved:** The main IC is identified as TI BQ79616PAPQ1.\n   167\t*   **Resolved:** `CB` and `VC` lines connect to the main IC.\n   168\t*   **Resolved:** `TSREF` source is U1 Pin 51.\n   169\t*   **Uncertainty:** The labels "BBN_CELL" and "BBP_CELL" in the Bus Bar section appear swapped relative to the port symbols. I have assumed the port symbols are correct.\n   170\t*   **Uncertainty:** The exact nature of `SRP_S`/`SRN_S`. They are likely "Sense Resistor Positive/Negative Sense" lines, but it is unclear if they come from a raw shunt or an amplifier. Given the direct connection to the BMS IC\'s auxiliary ADC inputs, they are likely raw shunt voltages (or slightly filtered), assuming the BMS IC has a suitable input range (usually +/- 200mV or similar for current sense).\n   171\t*   **Uncertainty:** R17 is DNP and placed across the `SRP_S`/`SRN_S` lines. It might be a placeholder for a differential filter resistor or a zero-ohm short for testing.\n   172\t*   **Uncertainty:** The purpose of U2 `INA` (Pin 3) being tied to GND via R123. It might be an unused channel or a configuration pin.\n   173\t*   **Uncertainty:** U2 `OUTA` (Pin 14) appears unconnected.\n   174\t*   **Resolved:** Q1 is active, Q2 is DNP. The NPN supply circuit is populated.\n   175\t*   **Conflict:** J3 pinout text vs schematic wires. Text says Pin 5 is TX, Pin 4 is RX. Schematic wires show Pin 4 is TX, Pin 3 is RX_C. Schematic wires are assumed correct.\n   176\t\n   177\t# Library Mapping\n   178\t\n   179\t| Designator | Component Name | Library Location |\n   180\t|------------|----------------|------------------|\n   181\t| U1 | BQ79616PAPR | /app/lib/imports/BQ79616PAPR.tsx |\n   182\t| U2 | ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 | /app/lib/imports/ISO7342FCQDWRQ1.tsx |\n   183\t| Q1 | MMBT3904LT1G | /app/lib/imports/MMBT3904LT1G.tsx |\n   184\t| D1 | 150060GS75000 | /app/lib/imports/A_150060GS75000.tsx |\n   185\t| D3 | BZX84C24 | /app/lib/imports/BZX84C24.tsx |\n   186\t| RT1-RT8 | NCP18XH103F03RB | /app/lib/imports/NCP18XH103F03RB.tsx |\n   187\t\n   188\t\n'}], 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_656cffc2356c4ef6a62216f2a9b0', 'name': 'file_editor'}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'Here\'s the result of running `cat -n` on /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/tscircuit_operation_manual.md:\n     1\t# `tscircuit` Operational Guide\n     2\t\n     3\t## Circuit Assembly, Modules & Connectivity\n     4\t\n     5\t---\n     6\t\n     7\t## Overview\n     8\t\n     9\tThis guide describes **how to assemble circuits using `tscircuit`** by:\n    10\t\n    11\t* instantiating existing components\n    12\t* organizing them into modules\n    13\t* arranging schematic and PCB layouts\n    14\t* defining connectivity using nets and traces\n    15\t\n    16\tIt assumes that **all components referenced already exist** and are importable.\n    17\t\n    18\t---\n    19\t\n    20\t## 1. Circuit Structure\n    21\t\n    22\t### Root Container\n    23\t\n    24\tEvery circuit must be wrapped in a `<board />` element.\n    25\t\n    26\t```tsx\n    27\t<board width="100mm" height="80mm">\n    28\t  {/* circuit contents */}\n    29\t</board>\n    30\t```\n    31\t\n    32\t* `<board />` is the root of schematic and PCB generation\n    33\t* All components, nets, groups, and traces must be children of `<board />`\n    34\t\n    35\t---\n    36\t\n    37\t## 2. Using Existing Components\n    38\t\n    39\t### A. Built-in Primitives\n    40\t\n    41\t`tscircuit` provides globally available primitives that **do not require imports**.\n    42\t\n    43\tCommon examples:\n    44\t\n    45\t* `<resistor />`\n    46\t* `<capacitor />`\n    47\t* `<inductor />`\n    48\t* `<diode />`\n    49\t* `<led />`\n    50\t* `<jumper />`\n    51\t* `<pinheader />`\n    52\t\n    53\t#### Required Props for Passives\n    54\t\n    55\t| Element         | Required Props                     |\n    56\t| --------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n    57\t| `<resistor />`  | `name`, `resistance`, `footprint`  |\n    58\t| `<capacitor />` | `name`, `capacitance`, `footprint` |\n    59\t| `<inductor />`  | `name`, `inductance`, `footprint`  |\n    60\t| `<led />`       | `name`, `color`, `footprint`       |\n    61\t\n    62\tExample:\n    63\t\n    64\t```tsx\n    65\t<resistor\n    66\t  name="R1"\n    67\t  resistance="10k"\n    68\t  footprint="0603"\n    69\t/>\n    70\t```\n    71\t\n    72\t---\n    73\t\n    74\t### B. Imported Components\n    75\t\n    76\tComponents defined elsewhere (e.g., ICs or reusable blocks) may be imported and instantiated.\n    77\t\n    78\t```tsx\n    79\timport { ControllerIC } from "./lib/ControllerIC"\n    80\t\n    81\t<ControllerIC name="U1" />\n    82\t```\n    83\t\n    84\t* Imported components are treated as black boxes\n    85\t* Pin names must be used exactly as exposed by the component\n    86\t\n    87\t---\n    88\t\n    89\t## 3. Nets\n    90\t\n    91\t### Declaring Nets\n    92\t\n    93\tNamed nets are used for shared electrical nodes.\n    94\t\n    95\t```tsx\n    96\t<net name="VCC" />\n    97\t<net name="GND" />\n    98\t```\n    99\t\n   100\t* Nets should be declared near the top of the `<board />`\n   101\t* Nets may be referenced anywhere within the board\n   102\t\n   103\t---\n   104\t\n   105\t### Connecting to Nets\n   106\t\n   107\tPins may be connected directly to nets.\n   108\t\n   109\t```tsx\n   110\t<trace from=".U1 > .VDD" to="net.VCC" />\n   111\t<trace from=".U1 > .GND" to="net.GND" />\n   112\t```\n   113\t\n   114\t---\n   115\t\n   116\t## 4. Connectivity\n   117\t\n   118\t### A. Point-to-Point Traces\n   119\t\n   120\tUse `<trace />` to connect two pins.\n   121\t\n   122\t```tsx\n   123\t<trace from=".U1 > .TX" to=".R1 > .pin1" />\n   124\t```\n   125\t\n   126\t* `from` and `to` use CSS-like selectors\n   127\t* Pin identifiers depend on the component definition\n   128\t\n   129\t---\n   130\t\n   131\t### B. Mixed Connectivity\n   132\t\n   133\tBoth nets and direct traces may coexist.\n   134\t\n   135\t```tsx\n   136\t<trace from=".C1 > .pin1" to="net.VCC" />\n   137\t<trace from=".C1 > .pin2" to="net.GND" />\n   138\t```\n   139\t\n   140\t---\n   141\t\n   142\t## 5. Modular Organization with `<group />`\n   143\t\n   144\t### Purpose\n   145\t\n   146\t`<group />` is a container for organizing related components and traces.\n   147\t\n   148\tIt may be used to:\n   149\t\n   150\t* cluster functionally related elements\n   151\t* simplify layout\n   152\t* move multiple elements together\n   153\t\n   154\t---\n   155\t\n   156\t### Auto-Layout Support\n   157\t\n   158\t`<group />` supports automatic layout for schematic and PCB.\n   159\t\n   160\t#### Schematic Layout Props\n   161\t\n   162\t* `schFlex`\n   163\t* `schFlexDirection="row" | "column"`\n   164\t* `schGap`\n   165\t\n   166\t#### PCB Layout Props\n   167\t\n   168\t* `pcbGrid`\n   169\t* `pcbGridCols`\n   170\t* `pcbGridGap`\n   171\t\n   172\t---\n   173\t\n   174\t### Example: Power Input Block\n   175\t\n   176\t```tsx\n   177\t<group\n   178\t  schFlex\n   179\t  schFlexDirection="row"\n   180\t  schGap="2mm"\n   181\t  pcbGrid\n   182\t  pcbGridCols={2}\n   183\t  pcbGridGap="1mm"\n   184\t>\n   185\t  <resistor name="R_Fuse" resistance="10" footprint="0603" />\n   186\t  <capacitor name="C_Bulk" capacitance="100uF" footprint="1206" />\n   187\t\n   188\t  <trace from=".R_Fuse > .pin2" to=".C_Bulk > .pos" />\n   189\t</group>\n   190\t```\n   191\t\n   192\t---\n   193\t\n   194\t## 6. Placement\n   195\t\n   196\t### Manual Placement\n   197\t\n   198\tComponents or groups may be positioned explicitly.\n   199\t\n   200\t* Schematic: `schX`, `schY`\n   201\t* PCB: `pcbX`, `pcbY`\n   202\t\n   203\t```tsx\n   204\t<capacitor\n   205\t  name="C1"\n   206\t  capacitance="100nF"\n   207\t  footprint="0402"\n   208\t  schX={10}\n   209\t  schY={5}\n   210\t  pcbX={12}\n   211\t  pcbY={8}\n   212\t/>\n   213\t```\n   214\t\n   215\t---\n   216\t\n   217\t### Placement Strategy\n   218\t\n   219\t* Manual coordinates are typically used at **top-level**\n   220\t* Auto-layout is preferred inside dense groups\n   221\t\n   222\t---\n   223\t\n   224\t## 7. Canonical Assembly Example\n   225\t\n   226\t```tsx\n   227\timport { ControllerIC } from "./lib/ControllerIC"\n   228\t\n   229\texport default () => {\n   230\t  return (\n   231\t    <board width="100mm" height="80mm">\n   232\t      <net name="VCC" />\n   233\t      <net name="GND" />\n   234\t\n   235\t      <ControllerIC name="U1" />\n   236\t\n   237\t      <capacitor\n   238\t        name="C_Decouple"\n   239\t        capacitance="100nF"\n   240\t        footprint="0402"\n   241\t      />\n   242\t\n   243\t      <trace from=".U1 > .VDD" to="net.VCC" />\n   244\t      <trace from=".U1 > .GND" to="net.GND" />\n   245\t      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin1" to="net.VCC" />\n   246\t      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin2" to="net.GND" />\n   247\t    </board>\n   248\t  )\n   249\t}\n   250\t```\n   251\t\n   252\t---\n   253\t\n   254\t## 8. Built-in Elements Reference\n   255\t\n   256\t| Element                           | Description               |\n   257\t| --------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n   258\t| `<board />`                       | Root container            |\n   259\t| `<net />`                         | Named electrical node     |\n   260\t| `<trace />`                       | Electrical connection     |\n   261\t| `<resistor />`                    | Passive resistor          |\n   262\t| `<capacitor />`                   | Passive capacitor         |\n   263\t| `<inductor />`                    | Passive inductor          |\n   264\t| `<diode />`                       | Diode                     |\n   265\t| `<led />`                         | LED                       |\n   266\t| `<jumper />` / `<solderjumper />` | Configuration links       |\n   267\t| `<pinheader />`                   | Connector                 |\n   268\t| `<group />`                       | Layout container          |\n   269\t| `<subcircuit />`                  | Isolated functional block |\n   270\t| `<via />`                         | PCB via                   |\n   271\t| `<testpoint />`                   | Measurement access        |\n   272\t| `<hole />`                        | Mechanical hole           |\n   273\t\n   274\t\n'}], 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_e50a4c341bb246969626eb9c64ec', 'name': 'file_editor'}], tools=[{'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'terminal', 'description': 'Execute a bash command in the terminal within a persistent shell session.\n\n\n### Command Execution\n* One command at a time: You can only execute one bash command at a time. If you need to run multiple commands sequentially, use `&&` or `;` to chain them together.\n* Persistent session: Commands execute in a persistent shell session where environment variables, virtual environments, and working directory persist between commands.\n* Soft timeout: Commands have a soft timeout of 10 seconds, once that\'s reached, you have the option to continue or interrupt the command (see section below for details)\n* Shell options: Do NOT use `set -e`, `set -eu`, or `set -euo pipefail` in shell scripts or commands in this environment. The runtime may not support them and can cause unusable shell sessions. If you want to run multi-line bash commands, write the commands to a file and then run it, instead.\n\n### Long-running Commands\n* For commands that may run indefinitely, run them in the background and redirect output to a file, e.g. `python3 app.py > server.log 2>&1 &`.\n* For commands that may run for a long time (e.g. installation or testing commands), or commands that run for a fixed amount of time (e.g. sleep), you should set the "timeout" parameter of your function call to an appropriate value.\n* If a bash command returns exit code `-1`, this means the process hit the soft timeout and is not yet finished. By setting `is_input` to `true`, you can:\n  - Send empty `command` to retrieve additional logs\n  - Send text (set `command` to the text) to STDIN of the running process\n  - Send control commands like `C-c` (Ctrl+C), `C-d` (Ctrl+D), or `C-z` (Ctrl+Z) to interrupt the process\n  - If you do C-c, you can re-start the process with a longer "timeout" parameter to let it run to completion\n\n### Best Practices\n* Directory verification: Before creating new directories or files, first verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location.\n* Directory management: Try to maintain working directory by using absolute paths and avoiding excessive use of `cd`.\n\n### Output Handling\n* Output truncation: If the output exceeds a maximum length, it will be truncated before being returned.\n\n### Terminal Reset\n* Terminal reset: If the terminal becomes unresponsive, you can set the "reset" parameter to `true` to create a new terminal session. This will terminate the current session and start fresh.\n* Warning: Resetting the terminal will lose all previously set environment variables, working directory changes, and any running processes. Use this only when the terminal stops responding to commands.\n', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The bash command to execute. Can be empty string to view additional logs when previous exit code is `-1`. Can be `C-c` (Ctrl+C) to interrupt the currently running process. Note: You can only execute one bash command at a time. If you need to run multiple commands sequentially, you can use `&&` or `;` to chain them together.'}, 'is_input': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'If True, the command is an input to the running process. If False, the command is a bash command to be executed in the terminal. Default is False.'}, 'timeout': {'type': 'number', 'description': 'Optional. Sets a maximum time limit (in seconds) for running the command. If the command takes longer than this limit, you’ll be asked whether to continue or stop it. If you don’t set a value, the command will instead pause and ask for confirmation when it produces no new output for 30 seconds. Use a higher value if the command is expected to take a long time (like installation or testing), or if it has a known fixed duration (like sleep).'}, 'reset': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'If True, reset the terminal by creating a new session. Use this only when the terminal becomes unresponsive. Note that all previously set environment variables and session state will be lost after reset. Cannot be used with is_input=True.'}, 'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['command', 'security_risk']}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'file_editor', 'description': "Custom editing tool for viewing, creating and editing files in plain-text format\n* State is persistent across command calls and discussions with the user\n* If `path` is an image file (.png, .jpg, .jpeg, .gif, .webp, .bmp), `view` displays the image content\n* If `path` is a text file, `view` displays the result of applying `cat -n`. If `path` is a directory, `view` lists non-hidden files and directories up to 2 levels deep\n* The `create` command cannot be used if the specified `path` already exists as a file\n* If a `command` generates a long output, it will be truncated and marked with `<response clipped>`\n* The `undo_edit` command will revert the last edit made to the file at `path`\n* This tool can be used for creating and editing files in plain-text format.\n\n\nBefore using this tool:\n1. Use the view tool to understand the file's contents and context\n2. Verify the directory path is correct (only applicable when creating new files):\n   - Use the view tool to verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location\n\nWhen making edits:\n   - Ensure the edit results in idiomatic, correct code\n   - Do not leave the code in a broken state\n   - Always use absolute file paths (starting with /)\n\nCRITICAL REQUIREMENTS FOR USING THIS TOOL:\n\n1. EXACT MATCHING: The `old_str` parameter must match EXACTLY one or more consecutive lines from the file, including all whitespace and indentation. The tool will fail if `old_str` matches multiple locations or doesn't match exactly with the file content.\n\n2. UNIQUENESS: The `old_str` must uniquely identify a single instance in the file:\n   - Include sufficient context before and after the change point (3-5 lines recommended)\n   - If not unique, the replacement will not be performed\n\n3. REPLACEMENT: The `new_str` parameter should contain the edited lines that replace the `old_str`. Both strings must be different.\n\nRemember: when making multiple file edits in a row to the same file, you should prefer to send all edits in a single message with multiple calls to this tool, rather than multiple messages with a single call each.\n\n\nYour current working directory is: /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary\nWhen exploring project structure, start with this directory instead of the root filesystem.", 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The commands to run. Allowed options are: `view`, `create`, `str_replace`, `insert`, `undo_edit`.', 'enum': ['view', 'create', 'str_replace', 'insert', 'undo_edit']}, 'path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Absolute path to file or directory.'}, 'file_text': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Required parameter of `create` command, with the content of the file to be created.'}, 'old_str': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Required parameter of `str_replace` command containing the string in `path` to replace.'}, 'new_str': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional parameter of `str_replace` command containing the new string (if not given, no string will be added). Required parameter of `insert` command containing the string to insert.'}, 'insert_line': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Required parameter of `insert` command. The `new_str` will be inserted AFTER the line `insert_line` of `path`.'}, 'view_range': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'integer'}, 'description': 'Optional parameter of `view` command when `path` points to a file. If none is given, the full file is shown. If provided, the file will be shown in the indicated line number range, e.g. [11, 12] will show lines 11 and 12. Indexing at 1 to start. Setting `[start_line, -1]` shows all lines from `start_line` to the end of the file.'}, 'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['command', 'path', 'security_risk']}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'VHL_Library_add_component', 'description': 'Add or update a TSX component or KiCAD footprint (.kicad_mod) in the local library. Validation is automatic.', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'component_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': "Logical name of the component (e.g. 'MyResistor') or filename with extension (e.g. 'footprint.kicad_mod')"}, 'file_content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Complete file content (TSX or KiCAD footprint)'}, 'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['component_name', 'file_content', 'security_risk']}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'VHL_Library_list_local_components', 'description': 'List components available in the local library.', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['security_risk']}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'VHL_Library_resolve_component_start', 'description': "PROCESS STEP 1: Start a component resolution process. This is a NON-BLOCKING operation that initiates a background process to resolve a hardware component. The process first checks the local library, then attempts to import from the global registry if not fou